      <h2 style="margin-top: 3rem;">Ways to Reduce Your Cost of Living in Spain</h2>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>Share housing:</strong> Renting a room in a shared apartment saves €200–€400/month vs renting alone.</li>
        <li><strong>Live outside city centers:</strong> Moving 15 minutes from downtown saves 25–40% on rent.</li>
        <li><strong>Use public transportation:</strong> €54/month unlimited travel. Most Spanish cities are transit-friendly.</li>
        <li><strong>Shop at markets and local shops:</strong> Weekly street markets have cheapest fresh produce. Carrefour and Lidl supermarkets are budget-friendly chains.</li>
        <li><strong>Eat menu del día:</strong> Set business lunches (€9–€13) are restaurant-quality food at budget prices.</li>
        <li><strong>Skip private healthcare:</strong> Public healthcare is free after residency registration. Avoid private insurance unless needed.</li>
        <li><strong>Take advantage of free activities:</strong> Many museums are free one evening per week. Beaches, parks, walking tours are free.</li>
        <li><strong>Use Airbnb for longer stays:</strong> Monthly Airbnb rates can be cheaper than traditional rent in some cities.</li>
        <li><strong>Embrace Spanish culture:</strong> Tapas bars, social dining culture, and free fiestas are built into life.</li>
        <li><strong>Consider digital nomad visa benefits:</strong> DNV allows you to work remotely, earning in strong currency while spending in weak euro.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 style="margin-top: 3rem;">What's NOT Expensive in Spain</h2>

      <p><strong>Healthcare:</strong> Free public system after residency. Prescriptions cost €0–€2.64 per item. Private doctor visits €50–€100 without insurance.</p>

      <p><strong>Education:</strong> Public schools free. Universities: €1,000–€3,000/year for EU citizens, more for non-EU.</p>

      <p><strong>Entertainment:</strong> Cinema €8–€10. Theater €15–€30. Live flamenco €20–€50. Clubs €15–€25 (often includes 1 drink).</p>

      <p><strong>Fitness:</strong> Gym membership €30–€50/month. Most beaches have free beach volleyball, running paths, outdoor gyms.</p>

      <p><strong>Food & Wine:</strong> Good wine €5–€10 per bottle. Craft beer €4–€6. Tapas €3–€8 each. Restaurant three-course dinner €15–€25.</p>

      <h2 style="margin-top: 3rem;">What IS Expensive in Spain</h2>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>Housing in Madrid/Barcelona city centers:</strong> €900–€1,500 for 1-bed apartment.</li>
        <li><strong>Imported goods:</strong> Non-European products (from US/Asia) cost 30–50% more due to import/shipping.</li>
        <li><strong>Car ownership:</strong> Petrol ~€1.40/liter, car insurance, registration. Public transport is cheaper.</li>
        <li><strong>International schools:</strong> €8,000–€15,000/year for private English-language schools.</li>
        <li><strong>Expat neighborhoods:</strong> Gràcia (Barcelona), Albufereta (Alicante), Pozuelo (Madrid)—tourist/expat areas cost 30–50% more.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 style="margin-top: 3rem;">Healthcare Costs for Expats</h2>

      <p>After registering as a resident (3–6 months in Spain), you qualify for free public healthcare (Sistema Nacional de Salud). This covers: doctor visits, hospitalizations, prescriptions, emergency care, preventive care. No copay system like private insurance; just a small prescription fee (usually €0–€2.64).</p>

      <p>Some expats prefer private insurance (€60–€150/month) for: shorter wait times, English-speaking doctors, access to private hospitals, coverage for things public system doesn't cover (dental, vision, cosmetic). Both options are affordable.</p>
