        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>What are the biggest living expenses in Spain?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>Largest monthly expenses: (1) Rent/Housing: €500–€1,500+ depending on city and apartment size, (2) Groceries/Food: €250–€400, (3) Utilities (electricity, water, internet): €100–€150, (4) Transport: €54/month for unlimited public transit, (5) Dining out/entertainment: €200–€500. Housing is typically 40–50% of total budget.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>How does Spain's cost of living compare to UK, US, Canada, Australia?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>Spain is significantly cheaper: (1) Spain is 30–50% cheaper than London, 25–40% cheaper than major US cities, 35–45% cheaper than Canada, and 40–50% cheaper than Australia. Housing is the biggest difference. A €600/month apartment in Valencia would cost £800+ in London, $1,200+ in US cities, CAD$1,100+ in Canada, AUD$1,400+ in Australia.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>What is the cheapest city to live in Spain?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>Cheapest cities: Granada, Córdoba, Almería, Jaén, and Cuenca offer the lowest costs (€900–€1,300/month total budget). These cities are charming, have good infrastructure, strong expat communities, and significantly lower housing costs (€400–€600 for decent apartments). They're good choices for people stretching limited budgets.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>Is healthcare free in Spain for expats?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>After registering as a resident (typically after 3–6 months in Spain), you can access Spain's public healthcare system (Seguridad Social) for free or minimal cost. This covers doctor visits, hospital care, prescriptions, and diagnostics. You must register at your local health center (centro de salud). Some expats prefer private insurance (€50–€150/month) for shorter wait times or English-speaking doctors.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>How much should you budget for dining out and entertainment in Spain?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>Dining out is affordable: Menu del día (set lunch): €9–€13, Dinner in mid-range restaurant: €15–€25 per person, Beers/drinks at bar: €2–€4. Monthly entertainment budget: €200–€400 for dining out 8–12 times monthly plus occasional concerts, cinema, activities. Spain's lifestyle culture means people dine out frequently, keeping costs down through competitive pricing.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
